## Exact accepted endpoint

The literal COMMON Bernoulli factor is jointly `ContDiffAt` in its two real parameters, and its negative-log response is jointly `ContDiffAt` wherever the literal factor is positive. The proof reuses the already accepted source definitions and Mathlib calculus. It also compiles five anonymous boundary applicability examples: p=0, q=1, q=0 with p<1 and nonzero exponent, exponent zero, and rejection of factor positivity at p=1,q=0 for nonzero exponent.

These are calculus statements. Two-sided ambient parameter neighborhoods do not weaken the positive original-source class or the strict ordinary-baseline requirement. No Taylor remainder, inverse-function construction, source witness, effective radius, complete acquisition, or general G3 decision theorem follows from this small profile alone.

